.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1205 PM PDT Mon Sep 28 2026
(This evening through Tuesday night)

The stratus has largely cleared out with some isolated patches
continuing at the immediate coast. Probability of stratus
development tonight is low to very low, as incoming ridging and
offshore flow will generally disrupt the marine layer stratus
formation processes.

The latest water vapor imagery shows an upper level low tracking
across the southern Central Valley into the Mojave Desert, expected
to continue trekking southeastward through the day and entering
Arizona by Tuesday morning. Behind the low, an incoming ridge
kickstarts a warming trend that will extend through the next couple
of days. Today`s high temperatures range from the middle 70s to
middle 80s inland to the upper 60s to middle 70s along the Bays and
the lower to middle 60s at the Pacific coast. On Tuesday, high
temperatures will reach the middle 80s to middle 90s inland, the
middle 70s to middle 80s along the Bays, and the middle 60s to lower
70s at the Pacific coast.

With the northerly flow aloft as the upper level low moves towards
the southeast, a push of offshore wind is expected in the higher
elevations Tuesday morning. Gusts of 25 to 35 mph are expected along
the exposed ridgelines, and stronger gusts are possible along the
ridge of the Vaca Mountains along the Napa-Yolo and Napa-Solano
county lines. Elsewhere, generally diurnal winds will prevail with
onshore winds developing each afternoon and evening, with gusts to
30 mph along the coast, through favored gaps and passes, and within
the Salinas Valley.

Minor coastal flooding is possible across the bayshore locations of
San Francisco Bay, San Pablo Bay, and Monterey Bay around high tide
starting today. Up to 1.2 feet of inundation above normally dry land
is expected, including up to 0.6 ft of surge caused by thermal
expansion and sea level rise since the sea level datum (basically
the point where we define as "sea level") was set in the 1983-2001
period. At the San Francisco tidal gauge, high tide is expected
to be 1.0 ft above normal (6.87 ft MLLW) at 12:57 PM Monday 9/28
and 1.2 ft above normal (7.01 ft MHHW) at 1:30 PM Tuesday 9/29.

