.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1205 PM PDT Mon Sep 28 2026
(Wednesday through next Sunday)

There is an additional push of higher elevation offshore flow on
Wednesday morning. This push will be more focused on the interior
mountains of the North and East Bay with gusts a little lighter than
those expected on Tuesday morning. Otherwise, temperatures may
fluctuate by a few degrees each day, most notably on Thursday when
stronger onshore flow will cause some of the coastal regions to dip
by a few degrees, but the general trend should remain similar to
Tuesday`s highs through the end of the seven day outlook. Areas of
Moderate HeatRisk, a level of heat that brings a moderate risk of
heat-related illnesses to sensitive populations, develop across the
interior regions Wednesday through Saturday, but confidence in
widespread impacts is not high enough to issue a Heat Advisory at
this point. Longer-range guidance suggests that temperatures above
the seasonal average will continue into the second week of October.

Minor coastal flooding around high tide continues in low-lying areas
across the San Francisco, San Pablo, and Monterey Bay shorelines
through Thursday and potentially into Friday. At the San Francisco
tidal gauge, high tide is expected to be 1.2 ft above normal (7.03
ft MHHW) at 2:10 PM Wednesday 9/30, and 1.1 ft above normal (6.92 ft
MHHW) at 2:58 PM on Thursday 10/1.

